1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:04,070
Chai Corner now has five
outlets and a small head office.

2
00:00:04,370 --> 00:00:07,417
But on winter evenings,
sales drop at all five.

3
00:00:07,967 --> 00:00:10,040
Kabir says, sell hot snacks.

4
00:00:10,040 --> 00:00:12,062
Asha says, stay open later.

5
00:00:12,362 --> 00:00:14,782
And Meena wants a new ginger flavour.

6
00:00:15,082 --> 00:00:18,365
Three ideas, and nobody
knows which one to pick.

7
00:00:19,015 --> 00:00:21,051
Picking by guesswork is risky.

8
00:00:21,051 --> 00:00:22,580
Ravi needs a process.

9
00:00:23,280 --> 00:00:25,113
So, what is decision making?

10
00:00:25,413 --> 00:00:30,430
Decision making means choosing the best
option from two or more alternatives.

11
00:00:31,030 --> 00:00:34,250
If there is only one way,
there is nothing to decide.

12
00:00:34,600 --> 00:00:39,292
And managers decide all day: what to
buy, who to hire, and when to open.

13
00:00:39,942 --> 00:00:42,199
Good decisions follow seven steps.

14
00:00:42,649 --> 00:00:43,549
One.

15
00:00:43,178 --> 00:00:44,715
Identify the problem.

16
00:00:44,715 --> 00:00:48,348
Evening sales are down by
a third, at every outlet.

17
00:00:48,698 --> 00:00:49,598
Two.

18
00:00:49,222 --> 00:00:50,579
Gather information.

19
00:00:50,579 --> 00:00:54,434
Kabir checks the sales, and
the team asks customers why.

20
00:00:54,785 --> 00:00:55,685
Three.

21
00:00:55,433 --> 00:01:00,160
Identify alternatives: hot snacks,
longer hours, or a new flavour.

22
00:01:00,510 --> 00:01:01,410
Four.

23
00:01:01,071 --> 00:01:02,401
Evaluate each one.

24
00:01:02,401 --> 00:01:04,152
Snacks are cheap to add.

25
00:01:04,152 --> 00:01:06,822
Longer hours mean paying staff at night.

26
00:01:06,822 --> 00:01:09,403
A new flavour takes time to catch on.

27
00:01:09,753 --> 00:01:10,653
Five.

28
00:01:10,303 --> 00:01:11,512
Choose the best.

29
00:01:11,512 --> 00:01:14,293
Hot snacks cost little and bring the most.

30
00:01:14,643 --> 00:01:15,543
Six.

31
00:01:15,201 --> 00:01:16,101
Implement.

32
00:01:16,087 --> 00:01:19,532
Hot snacks go on the
menu at all five outlets.

33
00:01:19,882 --> 00:01:20,782
And seven.

34
00:01:20,735 --> 00:01:21,635
Follow up.

35
00:01:21,588 --> 00:01:23,999
After one month, check the results.

36
00:01:24,649 --> 00:01:27,145
One month later, Kabir brings the numbers.

37
00:01:27,445 --> 00:01:30,141
Evening sales are back up at four outlets.

38
00:01:30,491 --> 00:01:34,157
But at the college outlet, students
say they want it open later.

39
00:01:34,507 --> 00:01:37,790
So Ravi starts the steps
again, for that one outlet.

40
00:01:37,790 --> 00:01:39,927
Follow up feeds the next decision.

41
00:01:40,577 --> 00:01:41,477
Quick check.

42
00:01:41,464 --> 00:01:44,051
Hot snacks are now on the menu everywhere.

43
00:01:44,051 --> 00:01:45,559
What is the next step?

44
00:01:47,459 --> 00:01:48,359
Follow up!

45
00:01:48,247 --> 00:01:51,643
Check the results, to see if
the decision really worked.

46
00:01:52,243 --> 00:01:53,256
So, remember.

47
00:01:53,556 --> 00:01:57,708
Decision making means choosing the
best option among alternatives.

48
00:01:58,058 --> 00:02:02,224
First, define the problem, gather
facts, list the options, and evaluate

49
00:02:02,224 --> 00:02:03,124
them.

50
00:02:03,074 --> 00:02:05,919
Then choose, implement,
and always follow up.

51
00:02:06,519 --> 00:02:10,915
Next time, we'll see why some decisions
are routine, and others are brand

52
00:02:10,915 --> 00:02:11,815
new.
